"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][23]={"school_physics_en:M23-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M23-L1 – Astrophysics: Stars and the Hertzsprung–Russell Diagram<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Optional and Advanced Topics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can two stars have the same surface temperature but emit very\ndifferent amounts of light?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>Hertzsprung–Russell diagram (HRD)<\/strong> places stars\naccording to <strong>luminosity<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(total radiated power, not apparent brightness in the sky) and effective\nsurface temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConventionally, temperature on the horizontal axis <strong>decreases to\nthe right<\/strong>; luminosity increases upwards, often logarithmically.\nIn a schematic HRD, the <strong>main sequence<\/strong> extends from hot\nand luminous (upper left) to cool and faint (lower right). Other regions\ncontain red giants and hot but comparatively faint white dwarfs. A\nstellar class does not uniquely determine age: stars of different\ninitial masses evolve along different pathways.<\/p>\n<p>For an approximate thermal emitter,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>σ<\/mi><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=4\\pi R^2\\sigma T^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the Stefan–Boltzmann constant. At fixed surface temperature, a larger\nradius implies greater luminosity. This helps explain the location of\ngiants compared with main-sequence stars. Inferring actual luminosities\nrequires distance measurements and corrections such as extinction;\napparent brightness alone is insufficient. The plotted points are\n<strong>invented teaching examples<\/strong>, not observed stars.\nAstrophysics is an <strong>optional profile<\/strong>, not a stand-alone\nnationally required German Abitur topic.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_139_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic luminosity versus surface temperature with a main sequence, giants and white dwarfs.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic luminosity versus surface\ntemperature with a main sequence, giants and white dwarfs.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic stellar populations on an HRD; temperature decreases to\nthe right; points are not measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Two idealised stars share the same surface temperature. Star B has\nthree times the radius of star A:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_B/L_A=(R_B/R_A)^2=3^2=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the model star B emits nine times the power, although their apparent\nranking in the sky could be reversed by different distances.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw HRD axes with temperature decreasing to the right. Mark a\nschematic main sequence, giants and white dwarfs. Explain which\nmeasurements and assumptions would be needed to classify a real\nstar.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is plotted on the vertical HRD axis?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which direction corresponds to higher temperature in the\nconventional HRD?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is luminosity different from apparent brightness?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Total radiated power, or luminosity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Towards the left.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It becomes four times larger.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Apparent brightness also depends on distance and attenuation along\nthe line of sight.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Ideal thermal radiation leaves out details of real stellar\natmospheres. Dust, composition, measurement uncertainty and evolution\naffect HRD positions; our plot contains no catalogue data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 18 and 20, §§2.4, 2.6:\nevaluation competence and optional extensions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy/pages/21-2-the-h-r-diagram-and-the-study-of-stellar-evolution\">OpenStax\n– Astronomy, §21.2 H–R Diagram<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §21.2: temperature, luminosity,\nmain sequence, stellar evolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M23-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M23-L2 – Special Relativity: Inertial Frames and Time Dilation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Optional and Advanced Topics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>How can observers assign different time intervals to the same pair of\nevents involving a moving clock?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Special relativity describes measurements in <strong>inertial\nframes<\/strong>, where force-free bodies move uniformly in straight\nlines. A central principle is that the vacuum speed of light\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis identical for inertial observers. Consequently, time intervals and\nclaims of simultaneity can depend on the observer’s frame. The interval\nbetween two events that occur <strong>at the same position of a clock\nmoving with them<\/strong> is called <strong>proper time<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For a clock moving at constant speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to another inertial frame, the appropriate comparison gives\n<strong>time dilation<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=\\gamma\\Delta\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1/\\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe clock’s rest frame measures\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\tau<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe other frame assigns the greater interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the same events. The formula applies for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq v&lt;c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin inertial motion. Events, synchronisation and frames must be\nidentified before comparing distant clocks. Under the KMK standards,\nrelativity is a <strong>possible extension<\/strong>, not a compulsory\nnational stand-alone lesson.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_140_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two schematic timelines show 8 microseconds proper time and 10 microseconds in another inertial frame for the same events.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two schematic timelines show 8\nmicroseconds proper time and 10 microseconds in another inertial frame\nfor the same events.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Time dilation at 0.60 c: two inertial frames assign different\nintervals to the same event pair.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.60c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1/\\sqrt{1-0.36}=1.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the moving clock records\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\tau=8.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween two events, the other inertial frame assigns\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=10.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nneither clock is faulty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw two event timelines. Label the two shared events, the proper\ntime and the interval in the other frame. Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.2c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.8c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nexplain why the Newtonian approximation works well at low speed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Under what condition is an interval proper time?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.8c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is time dilation not due to defective clocks?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The events take place at the same location in the clock’s rest\nframe.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1/\\sqrt{1-0.64}=5/3\\approx1.667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is a reproducible effect of spacetime structure, not a mechanical\nmalfunction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>This treatment assumes uniform inertial motion and ideal frame\nsynchronisation. Accelerated journeys require further reasoning; the\nschematic figure is not a scaled Minkowski diagram.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 18 and 20, §§2.4, 2.6:\nevaluation competence and optional extensions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/5-3-time-dilation\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §5.3 Time Dilation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §5.3: proper time,\nLorentz factor and frame intervals · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M23-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M23-L3 – Nuclear and Particle Physics: Interactions and Detector Tracks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Optional and Advanced Topics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>How can a detector reveal a charged particle without photographing\nthe particle itself?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Particle detectors register <strong>interactions<\/strong> of\nparticles with detector materials, not tiny photographic images of the\nparticles. In a cloud chamber droplets condense along ionisation trails;\nmodern tracking detectors reconstruct trajectories from electronic hits.\nA magnetic field can curve the path of a charged particle. For motion\nperpendicular to a uniform magnetic field, the magnitude satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=|q|Br<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis momentum,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncharge magnitude,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmagnetic flux density and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncurvature radius. The curvature direction depends on charge sign,\ndirection of travel and magnetic-field direction. Parallel travel causes\nno magnetic Lorentz force.<\/p>\n<p>An event display presents <strong>reconstructed evidence<\/strong>. A\ntrack by itself does not identify a particle: mass, energy loss,\ndetector hits and complementary information are relevant. A decay may\nhave tracks meeting at a vertex; neutral particles often leave no curved\ntrack but can produce charged daughter particles. The branching diagram\nis an <strong>instructional schematic<\/strong>, not an actual collision,\na Feynman diagram or a calibrated event reconstruction. The KMK names\nnuclear and particle physics as an <strong>optional\nextension<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_141_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two invented curved tracks illustrate distinct charged-particle curvature radii in a magnetic field.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two invented curved tracks illustrate\ndistinct charged-particle curvature radii in a magnetic\nfield.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Larger radius implies greater transverse momentum at fixed charge\nmagnitude and field.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Two particles with equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntravel perpendicular to the same uniform field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=0.50\\,\\mathrm T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.40\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.204<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=|q|Br=3.204\\times10^{-20}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDouble the radius would imply double the momentum under the same field\nand charge assumptions. This is a model example, not an identification\nof a real particle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Compare two invented, labelled curved tracks in a constant field.\nIdentify the larger momentum and explain why the charge magnitude and\nfield value are needed to determine its absolute magnitude. Analyse\npublic CERN educational examples instead of building radioactive or\ncryogenic detectors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a detector measure directly?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a larger radius imply at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|q|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why may a neutral particle lack an immediately visible curved\ntrack?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is curvature alone enough to find the particle mass?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Signals such as ionisation or electronic hits in detector\nmaterials.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Greater transverse momentum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Without charge it does not curve magnetically, although charged\ndecay products may be detected.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; complementary measurements are required.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The circular model assumes a uniform magnetic field, small energy\nloss and a suitable trajectory angle. Real experiments require\ncalibration and complex track reconstruction; our drawing contains no\nmeasured event.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 18 and 20, §§2.4, 2.6:\nevaluation competence and optional extensions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://home.cern/science/experiments/how-detector-works/\">CERN –\nHow a Detector Works<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Detector signals and momentum inferred from\ntrack curvature · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M23-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M23-L4 – Climate Physics: Albedo and Planetary Energy Balance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Optional and Advanced Topics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a small change in reflected solar radiation matter for\nEarth’s energy balance?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>planetary energy budget<\/strong> compares incoming\nshortwave sunlight, reflected radiation and outgoing longwave thermal\nradiation. At the top of the atmosphere, global and time-averaged\nincoming solar radiation is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=340\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA fraction described by <strong>albedo<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis reflected. In a simplified stationary model, the absorbed flux is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{abs}}=(1-\\alpha)S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand at equilibrium\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{out}}=F_{\\mathrm{abs}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha\\approx0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis yields about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>238<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">238\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nNASA cites approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">240\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the average absorption.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>greenhouse effect<\/strong> does not generate energy.\nGreenhouse gases affect the altitudes and spectral bands at which Earth\nradiates longwave energy to space, changing the overall energy balance.\nA single top-of-atmosphere budget with no distinct atmospheric and\nsurface layers does <strong>not<\/strong> determine surface temperature.\nUnder a blackbody assumption, the effective emission temperature follows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\sigma T_{\\mathrm{eff}}^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nActual climate evolution includes spatial patterns, time dependence,\nspectral behaviour and feedbacks. Climate physics is a possible KMK\nadvanced-course extension.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_142_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic arrows show 340 watts per square metre incoming, 102 reflected and 238 thermal output at equilibrium.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic arrows show 340 watts per\nsquare metre incoming, 102 reflected and 238 thermal output at\nequilibrium.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Simplified global-mean energy budget, not a surface or regional\nenergy balance.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=340\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.70<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>238<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{abs}}=0.70\\times340=238\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>231.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">231.2\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-6.8\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>holding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfixed<\/strong> – an instantaneous model comparison, not a real-world\ntemperature forecast.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Create an original flow schematic with 340 W/m² incoming, 102 W/m²\nreflected and 238 W/m² absorbed solar radiation. Mark these as globally\naveraged teaching values. Explain why outgoing radiation can temporarily\ndiffer from absorption as the climate system warms or cools.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does albedo mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate absorption for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=340<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a difference between incoming and outgoing radiation\nmatter?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can ground surface temperature be read directly from this simple\ntop-of-atmosphere budget?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The reflected fraction of incident shortwave radiation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>255<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.75\\times340=255\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A non-zero imbalance implies changing energy stored in the Earth\nsystem.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; atmosphere, spectral processes and heat transport are also\nneeded.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Values are rounded global annual averages. The illustration concerns\nonly the top-of-atmosphere budget, not local fluxes or a complete\nsurface budget; incoming radiation and albedo vary across time and\nplace.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 18 and 20, §§2.4, 2.6:\nevaluation competence and optional extensions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/climate-and-earths-energy-budget/\">NASA\n– Climate and Earth’s Energy Budget<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Global incoming and absorbed\nflux, reflection and budget limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M23-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M23-L5 – Solid-State Physics: Energy Bands and Semiconductors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Optional and Advanced Topics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a semiconductor conduct differently from a metal or an\ninsulator?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In a <strong>solid<\/strong>, interactions between many atoms create\nallowed <strong>energy bands<\/strong> rather than isolated atomic energy\nlevels. In a simplified model a completely filled <strong>valence\nband<\/strong> contributes little to transport; mobile electrons in the\n<strong>conduction band<\/strong> and missing valence electrons (holes)\ncan carry charge. The energy separation is the <strong>band gap<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMetals have accessible states near the Fermi energy; insulators\ntypically have a large band gap, while semiconductors have a smaller\none. These are qualitative categories rather than a sharp universal\nnumerical boundary.<\/p>\n<p>Thermal energy or sufficiently energetic photons can excite electrons\nacross the gap, generating electron–hole pairs. For an ideal photon\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=hc/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E\\ge E_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a necessary energy condition for interband excitation; transition\nprobabilities and momentum conservation still depend on band structure.\n<strong>Doping<\/strong> modifies available charge carriers. A\nfunctioning photovoltaic cell additionally needs suitable internal\nfields and contacts to separate and extract carriers. The diagram shows\nschematic energy levels, not measured silicon data. Solid-state physics\nis an optional KMK extension.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_143_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic valence and conduction bands with a band gap and electron–hole excitation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic valence and conduction bands\nwith a band gap and electron–hole excitation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Electronic excitation in an ideal semiconductor; band positions\nare not to scale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An ideal direct band gap\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_g=1.50\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to threshold wavelength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>827<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_g\\approx1240\\,\\mathrm{eV\\,nm}/1.50\\,\\mathrm{eV}=827\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>700<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">700\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphoton has about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.77<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.77\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nenergetically above threshold. This does <strong>not<\/strong> imply that\nevery photon is absorbed or produces useful electric current.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw separate valence and conduction bands with a gap, an excitation\narrow and a hole. Compare two hypothetical materials having\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_g=1.5\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand calculate their corresponding limiting wavelengths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the band model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name the two main bands shown.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_g=3.0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the band gap alone insufficient to predict solar-cell\nefficiency?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A model of allowed electron energies in a crystal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Valence band and conduction band.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1240<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>413<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_g\\approx1240/3.0=413\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Absorption, recombination, contacts, the input spectrum and other\nlosses affect efficiency.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The flat horizontal bands and single excitation arrow simplify real\nenergy–momentum dispersion. Silicon has an indirect gap; the example is\na fictional direct-gap model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 18 and 20, §§2.4, 2.6:\nevaluation competence and optional extensions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/9-5-band-theory-of-solids\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §9.5 Band Theory of Solids<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §9.5: valence\nband, conduction band and band gap · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M23-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M23-L6 – Physics and Society: Evidence, Risks and Decisions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Optional and Advanced Topics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Is a high efficiency figure alone sufficient to decide which\ntechnology best serves society?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>physical finding<\/strong> is a testable measurement or\nmodel outcome, such as energy efficiency. An <strong>evaluation<\/strong>\nalso requires criteria such as costs, material requirements, safety,\navailability, social consequences and time horizon. Selecting the\nlargest number from a table may ignore incompatible system boundaries. A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nloss at device level is not necessarily a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nloss across an entire supply chain. Technical claims therefore need a\n<strong>quantity and unit, source and date, system boundary and\nuncertainty<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p>A transparent evaluation first defines the decision and options, then\nseparates verifiable numerical evidence from value judgements.\n<strong>Criterion weights<\/strong> are declared, sensitivity is tested\nand missing data are recorded as missing. Weighted scores are decision\naids, not physical proofs. Two people can accurately use the same\nmeasurements but reach different conclusions because they legitimately\nprioritize different outcomes. <strong>Evaluation competence<\/strong> is\nmandatory in the KMK framework; selecting particular technologies as\nexamples is optional.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_144_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two bar panels illustrate a ranking reversal between fictional systems when weights change.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two bar panels illustrate a ranking\nreversal between fictional systems when weights change.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Fictional scoring matrix: changing weights alters the ranking\nwithout new measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Two <strong>purely fictional<\/strong> systems A and B receive 0–10\n<strong>scores<\/strong> (higher = preferred) for efficiency and low\nmaterial demand: A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(9,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nB\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(7,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.7,0.3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_A=0.7\\times9+0.3\\times4=7.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_B=0.7\\times7+0.3\\times8=7.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nA is slightly preferred. Changing weights to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.4,0.6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_A=6.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_B=7.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreversing the result. These <strong>scores are invented<\/strong>, not\nmeasurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Create a criteria matrix for an energy-related decision. Find at\nleast two actual dated technical sources and explicitly identify missing\ndata; use the labelled fictional scores for the initial arithmetic.\nRecalculate with changed weights. Include an evidence-based opposing\nview and one consequence for affected people.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What distinguishes a measured finding from a value judgement?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is B’s score for weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.7,0.3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a reversed ranking under different weights reveal?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name four details needed to compare a technical number fairly.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Findings can be independently tested; priorities and trade-offs\ninvolve normative criteria.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>7.3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The choice depends on priorities and cannot be determined by physics\nalone.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Quantity/unit, system boundary, source/date and uncertainty.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The matrix is not a life-cycle study or a political recommendation.\nScales and weights are constructed; real decisions require verified\ndata, side-effect analysis and affected stakeholders.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M24&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M24-L1 – Demand Levels and Task\nOperators<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · pp. 18 and 20, §§2.4, 2.6:\nevaluation competence and optional extensions · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/climate-and-earths-energy-budget/\">NASA\n– Climate and Earth’s Energy Budget<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Global incoming and absorbed\nflux, reflection and budget limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/9-5-band-theory-of-solids\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §9.5 Band Theory of Solids<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §9.5: valence\nband, conduction band and band gap · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
